---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/berkeley-woman.json";
---
<Base
  title="Hannah was a Berkeley woman"
  eyebrow={`1851 census · found ${d.found}`}
  dek="The oldest open question in this archive is closed, and the answer is the one the family tree gave at the start. Everything built on top of the opposite answer is wrong, and it is all still on the site.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">For as long as this archive has existed, one sentence has organised its English
    research: <strong>Hannah Saniger was not born in Gloucestershire.</strong> It came from a tick in
    a column of the 1841 census. It sent the search to Bristol's chapels, to the Somerset side of the
    Avon, and finally to a parish called Chew Magna.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">The 1851 census was taken in the same house, about the same woman,
    ten years later. It did not ask whether she was born in this county. It asked <em>where</em>.</p>

    <Note style="border-left-width:3px">
      <strong style="font-size:1.15em">Birth town: BERKELEY. Birth county: GLOUCESTERSHIRE.</strong>
      <br /><br />
      Which is what <a href={u("/people/hannah-saniger/")}>the family tree</a> said before any of this
      began. <span class="chip doc">documented</span>
    </Note>
  </section>

  <Blk label={d.census} title="The household, complete">

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Relation</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Born</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.household.map(([fn, sn, rel, ms, sx, age, by, occ, bp]) => (
            <tr style={fn === "Hannah" ? "background:var(--tint)" : ""}>
              <td><strong>{fn}</strong> <span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{sn}</span></td>
              <td class="small">{rel}</td>
              <td class="m">{age}</td>
              <td class="small">{occ}</td>
              <td class="small">{bp === "—" ? <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">—</span> : <strong>{bp}</strong>}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label={d.marriage.set} title="And the marriage, at last">
    <p class="lede">{d.marriage.line}.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Residence: {d.marriage.residence}.</p>
    <Note>{d.marriage.means}
      <span class="chip doc">documented in England</span></Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="What this cost" title="An edifice on a tick">

    <p class="lede">The claim was: <em>{d.wrong.claim}</em> On it this archive built:</p>
    <ol class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      {d.wrong.built.map((x) => (<li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">{x}</li>))}
    </ol>

    <Note>
      <strong>All of it rested on one thing.</strong> {d.wrong.rested}
      <br /><br />
      {d.wrong.beats}
      <br /><br />
      A binary tick is the weakest statement a census makes, and this archive treated it as the
      strongest thing it had — because it was the only thing it had, and because nobody tested it
      against the next census. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Filed as the largest error here →</a>
    </Note>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">The pages built on the wrong answer are still up, marked
    wrong rather than deleted, because how an archive goes wrong is part of what it knows.
    <a href={u("/chew-magna/")}>Chew Magna →</a> · <a href={u("/bristol-chapels/")}>The Bristol chapels →</a></p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Four things nobody here knew this morning" title="What else the page gives">
    <ol class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      {d.newFacts.map(([h, b]) => (<li style="margin-bottom:.7rem"><strong>{h}.</strong> {b}</li>))}
    </ol>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Stroud, 6 April 1806" title="And a whole family nobody here had a name for">

    <p class="lede">{d.wakefield.baptism}</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">{d.wakefield.means}</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>{d.wakefield.webb}</strong>
      <a href={u("/drivers-fields/")}>The 1841 household →</a>
      <span class="chip inf">an identification</span>
      <br /><br />
      {d.wakefield.caveat}
    </Note>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.wakefield.jabez}</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>Correction, made the same day.</strong> {d.queensland}
      <a href={u("/general-hewitt/")}>The manifest →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Filed →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Bristol, St Paul, Portland Square" title="Ellen was not left behind">

    <p class="lede">{d.ellen.baptism}</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:.9rem">{d.ellen.burial}</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>{d.ellen.means}</strong> <span class="chip doc">documented</span>
      <br /><br />
      {d.ellen.eliza}
      <br /><br />
      {d.ellen.lesson} <a href={u("/general-hewitt/")}>The page that framed it →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Coverage, tested" title="Why Hannah's own baptism is still missing">

    <p><strong>The test.</strong> {d.hannahGap.test} <strong>The result.</strong>
    {d.hannahGap.result}</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>{d.hannahGap.verdict}</strong>
      <a href={u("/hannah-baptism")}>The record →</a>
      <span class="chip sup">superseded</span>
      <br /><br />
      {d.hannahGap.next}
    </Note>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.lydia}
      <a href={u("/drivers-fields/")}>Lyddya Sinegar →</a></p>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>And the gap that was never a gap</h2>
    <p class="lede">{d.berkeley}</p>
    <Note>
      <strong>This is the real lesson, and it is not about Hannah.</strong> Every null result on this
      site was measured against free indexes, and
      <a href={u("/coverage/")}>the page that measures them</a> now needs a line at the top: the
      commonest reason a record could not be found here is that it was behind a subscription. The
      coverage discipline was right. The conclusion drawn from it — that the record did not exist in
      reach — was a statement about this archive's budget, not about the archives of England.
    </Note>
  </section>
</Base>
